{
  "statement": "No, I do not know, and the read was cut short are three different sentences. No is said only after a complete read; a ticked row is found and reported as done; a read that was truncated says what it saw and never a no; not knowing is one plain sentence with no guess, no apology and no suggestion; and a device preference is something offload has never held.",
  "why": "A wrong no is somebody buying butter twice. Absence asserted from a shortlist that did not reach the end is a guess wearing the shape of an answer, and a guess at their theme is a guess about something offload cannot see.",
  "sources": [
    "013 AC-4, AC-4a, AC-4b, AC-4c, AC-4d, AC-5c, AC-7",
    "answer.md: Saying no, and saying you do not know, are different sentences"
